+# Invoicing
+
+This library turns a customer order into an invoice: one priced line per
+item, plus any shipping and rounding lines, and the invoice-level totals.
+
+## How invoice totals are calculated
+
+### Rounding
+
+Every amount is rounded to the currency's smallest unit: the nearest cent for
+USD and EUR, the nearest whole yen for JPY (for example $1.4985 → $1.50).
+An amount that is exactly halfway normally rounds up (¥2.5 → ¥3), but see
+the note on halfway amounts at the end of this section.
+
+### Inputs
+
+An order carries:
+
+- the **items** bought, each with a quantity and a unit price;
+- an **order discount rate** (e.g. 15%) that applies to every item;
+- optionally, an **item discount rate** on individual items. It *replaces*
+  the order discount rate for that item only (it is not added to it). An item
+  rate of 0% means that item gets no discount at all;
+- a **tax rate** (e.g. 8.25%);
+- optionally, a flat **shipping fee**.
+
+### Each item line
+
+For every item, calculated in this order and rounded at each step:
+
+| Column   | How it is calculated                                                     |
+| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| Gross    | quantity × unit price, rounded                                           |
+| Discount | gross × discount rate (item rate if set, otherwise order rate), rounded |
+| Tax      | (gross − discount) × tax rate, rounded                                   |
+| Total    | gross − discount + tax                                                   |
+
+Tax is always charged on the discounted amount, never on the full price.
+
+### Invoice totals
+
+The invoice totals are **not** built by adding up the line columns. They are
+calculated once for the whole order, rounding only at the end of each step:
+
+| Total         | How it is calculated                                                                                    |
+| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| Subtotal      | sum of quantity × unit price over all items, rounded                                                    |
+| Discount total| sum of (quantity × unit price × that item's discount rate) over all items, rounded once                 |
+| Tax total     | (subtotal − discount total) × tax rate, rounded                                                         |
+| Shipping total| the shipping fee, rounded; 0 if there is no fee (a zero or negative fee is ignored)                      |
+| Total         | subtotal − discount total + tax total + shipping total                                                  |
+
+Shipping is never discounted and never taxed, and it is not part of the
+subtotal.
+
+### Kinds of line on an invoice
+
+An invoice can contain three kinds of line, always in this order:
+
+1. **Item lines** (one per item on the order, in order). These are the
+   products bought, priced as described above.
+2. **Shipping line** (SKU `SHIP`, at most one). Present only when the order
+   has a shipping fee above zero. Its amount is the shipping fee, with zero
+   discount and zero tax.
+3. **Rounding adjustment line** (SKU `ADJ`, at most one). Each item line is
+   rounded on its own, while the invoice total is rounded once for the whole
+   order. Those two ways of rounding can land a few cents apart. When they
+   do, an adjustment line is added for exactly the difference, so that the
+   lines always add up to the invoice total. It can be positive or negative,
+   and it can be more than one cent on invoices with many lines. It has no
+   discount or tax split — it is a single amount.
+
+### How the lines relate to the invoice total
+
+- The **line totals always add up exactly to the invoice total** — the
+  adjustment line guarantees this.
+- The individual line **discount** and **tax** columns do *not* necessarily
+  add up to the invoice's discount total and tax total. They can differ by a
+  few cents for the same rounding reason; that difference ends up in the
+  adjustment line's total. The invoice-level figures (subtotal, discount
+  total, tax total, total) are the authoritative ones.
+
+### Worked example
+
+Order in USD with a 15% order discount, 8.25% tax and a $7.50 shipping fee:
+
+| Item       | Qty | Unit price |
+| ---------- | --: | ---------: |
+| Coffee mug |   1 |      $9.99 |
+| Hoodie     |   1 |     $29.99 |
+| Boots      |   2 |     $49.99 |
+
+**Lines**
+
+| Line       | Gross   | Discount (15%)             | Tax (8.25%)                        | Total   |
+| ---------- | ------: | -------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------: |
+| Coffee mug |   $9.99 | 9.99 × 0.15 = 1.4985 → $1.50 | (9.99 − 1.50) × 0.0825 = 0.7004 → $0.70 |   $9.19 |
+| Hoodie     |  $29.99 | 29.99 × 0.15 = 4.4985 → $4.50 | (29.99 − 4.50) × 0.0825 = 2.1029 → $2.10 |  $27.59 |
+| Boots      |  $99.98 | 99.98 × 0.15 = 14.997 → $15.00 | (99.98 − 15.00) × 0.0825 = 7.0109 → $7.01 |  $91.99 |
+| Shipping   |   $7.50 | $0.00                      | $0.00                              |   $7.50 |
+| Rounding adjustment | | |                                         |   $0.02 |
+| **Sum of lines** | | **$21.00**                  | **$9.81**                          | **$136.29** |
+
+**Invoice totals**
+
+| Total          | Calculation                                   | Amount      |
+| -------------- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------: |
+| Subtotal       | 9.99 + 29.99 + 99.98                          |    $139.96  |
+| Discount total | 139.96 × 0.15 = 20.994 → rounded              |     $20.99  |
+| Tax total      | (139.96 − 20.99) × 0.0825 = 9.8150 → rounded  |      $9.82  |
+| Shipping total |                                               |      $7.50  |
+| **Total**      | 139.96 − 20.99 + 9.82 + 7.50                  | **$136.29** |
+
+The three item lines plus shipping come to $136.27, two cents short of the
+$136.29 total, so a $0.02 rounding adjustment line is added. Note also that
+the line discounts add up to $21.00 and the line taxes to $9.81, while the
+invoice shows a discount total of $20.99 and a tax total of $9.82 — that is
+the rounding difference described above.
+
+### Note on halfway amounts
+
+Because of how computers store decimal numbers, some amounts that look exactly
+halfway to a cent round down instead of up (for example $1.005 becomes $1.00).
+A negative amount that is exactly halfway rounds towards zero (−¥2.5 → −¥2).
+These cases are rare in practice, but they can explain a one-cent difference
+from a hand calculation.
+
+## Development
+
+Tests are run with:
+
+```sh
+node --experimental-strip-types --test "tests/**/*.test.ts"
+```
